---
// Stay safe (spec 6.1): never pay to apply, red flags, how to check an employer, and
// what to do if someone asks for money. Linked from every page's footer.
import Banner from '../components/Banner.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Stay safe: how to spot a fake job"
  description="Never pay to apply. Red flags of fake graduate jobs in Nigeria, how to check an employer is real, and what to do if someone asks you for money."
  current="stay-safe"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Stay safe: how to spot a fake job</h1>
    <Banner kind="warning" title="Never pay to apply">
      <p>
        Real employers do not charge application, "processing", training, medical or uniform fees to
        hire you. If you are asked to pay to get a job or to "secure" a place, it is a scam.
      </p>
    </Banner>

    <h2>Red flags</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Any request for money</strong>, before or after an "offer", by transfer, card or
        airtime.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A recruiter who only uses Gmail, Yahoo or WhatsApp</strong> for a big company. Real
        companies usually write from their own domain (for example name@company.com).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pay that is far too good</strong> for a graduate role, or a job offered with no
        interview.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pressure to act now</strong>: "only today", "slots are filling up", "don't tell
        anyone".
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Requests for your BVN, bank login, card PIN or OTP.</strong> No employer needs these
        to hire you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An interview in a hotel, a house or another odd place</strong>, or late at night.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spelling mistakes, a fake-looking website</strong>, or a web address that is close
        to a real company's but not quite right.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to check an employer is real</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Find the company's official website yourself (not from the message) and look for the opening
        on its careers page.
      </li>
      <li>Check that emails come from the company's own domain, not a free email address.</li>
      <li>Search the company's name with words like "scam" or "fake" and read what others say.</li>
      <li>If you can, call the company on a number from its official website and ask.</li>
      <li>
        Ask someone you trust, such as a lecturer, a career adviser or a friend in the industry.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>If someone asks you for money</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Stop. Don't send money, and don't share your bank details, card, BVN or OTP.</li>
      <li>Keep the messages, emails and account numbers as evidence.</li>
      <li>If you have already paid, contact your bank straight away.</li>
      <li>Report it to the platform where you saw the opening, and to the police or the EFCC.</li>
      <li>Warn your friends, so the same people can't catch them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we help</h2>
    <p>
      We check openings before listing them and leave out ones we can't confirm. Every listing shows
      when we last checked it. But we can't guarantee any employer, so always confirm on the
      organisation's own page. We don't hire or process applications.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/">See checked jobs</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: var(--border-thin) solid var(--color-rule);
  }
  ul,
  ol {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
  }
  .prose > p > a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--tap-min);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }
</style>
